proxy_service: bound the hourly refresh, and keep its thread alive

PeriodicRefresh re-fetched patch and unlocks for every cached game an hour
after every pass, with no age test, no slice and no cooldown -- sized, as
the rest of the client was, under MAX_CACHED_GAMES = 100: two hundred GETs
an hour at the very most. ROCKNIX lifts that cap (its patch 004; the
maintainer's call is that every game on the console earns offline), and
the same loop over a 1,000-game library is 2,000 GETs each online hour,
about 41,000 a day per device, ten minutes of back-to-back requests at the
0.3 s throttle -- from a User-Agent RetroAchievements registered for a
casual-only client, on every device that turns the feature on. That is
the cost the cap was protecting, deleted with the cap (ROCKNIX audit #186
PL-01, High).

A pass now refreshes only the games whose patch row is at least
REFRESH_MIN_AGE_SECONDS (24 h) old, the oldest first, and at most
REFRESH_MAX_GAMES_PER_PASS (50) of them. The arithmetic, per device:

    before: 2N GETs an hour                 N=100: 200/h, 4,800/day
                                            N=1,000: 2,000/h, 41,000/day
    after:  2*min(N,50) GETs an hour        any N: <= 100/h, <= 2,400/day
            each game about once a day up to 1,200 games, once every
            N/50 hours beyond -- a 1,000-game library every ~20 h

so the patched service asks less than the capped client's own worst case,
for any library. The choice of a daily, oldest-first slice over "recently
played only" is the cheapest form that keeps every cached game's unlocks
within a day of the site's (D-CLOUD-034); the maintainer can narrow it to
the client's content history (smart_cache.load_content_history_paths) if
a day is too often for games nobody plays. select_games_to_refresh is a
pure function so the predicate can be pinned by a test.

Second, the thread had no exception boundary: refresh_game_patch raises
CacheGameError on any network or API failure, and one such raised out of
run(), ended the daemon thread, and the service ran on with no refresh and
no eviction until its next start -- Restart=on-failure never sees a dead
thread (audit #186 PL-25). A pass now catches per game: a refused sign-in
(CacheGameAuthError) ends the pass without a request for every remaining
game, three failures in a row end it, anything else is logged and the
next game is tried; and run() catches around the whole pass, so the next
pass runs an hour later whatever happened in this one.

Written for ROCKNIX (fork issue maxengel/rocknix#186, PL-01 and PL-25).
Offered upstream as-is (ROCKNIX D-RA-016: whatever the fork changes in
the proxy goes back).

